::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
MGWR let every term in the local regression of **Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100)** operate at its own spatial scale (variables z-standardized, 343 units); overall R² is 0.629.

Selected bandwidths: **the intercept** at 44 nearest neighbors (local); **Urbanization rate (% of population)** at 90 nearest neighbors (local). Smaller bandwidths mean the association shifts over shorter distances.

The **Urbanization rate (% of population)** association varies locally from 0.215 to 1.01; it is strongest around **Ravelo** (local coefficient 1.01). 326 of 343 units clear the corrected threshold (|t| ≥ 2.83); non-significant units are greyed on the map.

Coefficients are on the standardized scale (MGWR requires z-standardized variables), so magnitudes are comparable across terms; significance uses per-term corrected alphas.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
:::
:::


The residuals are in standard deviations of the index: positive where the index is higher than the model gives. Look for groups of neighbours with the same sign, which would mean that the model still misses something that varies over space. Moran's I measures it ([Maps and spatial dependence](spatial-dependence.qmd)):

::: {#03af10b8 .cell execution_count=16}
``` {.python .cell-code}
diag = geo.spatial_diagnostics(z, "index_sdg", "urbanization")
left = geo.moran(res.df, "residual")
print("Moran's I of the residuals. Least squares:", round(diag.moran_i_resid, 2),
      "| MGWR:", round(left.moran_i, 2), "(p =", left.p_sim, ")")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Moran's I of the residuals. Least squares: 0.47 | MGWR: 0.15 (p = 0.001 )
```
:::
:::


The spatial pattern in the residuals falls from 0.47 to 0.15. Most of it is gone, but not all: some clustering among neighbours remains.

## Two regressors: poverty, the index and urbanization

With more than one regressor, each gets its own bandwidth, and they can differ a lot. The second model regresses multidimensional poverty on the index and urbanization.

::: {#7d3b466b .cell execution_count=17}
``` {.python .cell-code}
res2 = geo.mgwr(df, "sdg1_2_mpi", ["index_sdg", "urbanization"])
res2.gt_bw
```

